#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t0-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:4.4.t38
REASONING=Structure: "quod si ... sentiam" is the protasis (dependent, introduced by *si*, with *sentiam* as its verb); "perficiam" is the single independent verb; "profecto" modifies it; "ut ... putetis" is the result/substantive clause dependent on *perficiam*; inside it *putetis* governs two indirect statements about "hunc A. Licinium": (a) "non modo non segregandum [esse] ... a numero civium", and (b) "verum etiam ... asciscendum fuisse". Infinitives: *tribui* and *concedi* are passive infinitives in indirect statement under *sentiam*, joined by the enclitic *-que* (which therefore links the two infinitives, not the surrounding nouns). Gerundives: per the stated convention, *segregandum* and *asciscendum* are adjectives (adjectival to *Licinium*), so the form of *sum* carries the verbal expression. With *asciscendum* the real infinitive *fuisse* anchors it; with *segregandum* the infinitive *esse* is elided, so I add an implied-sum token (4.t19_implied) immediately after *segregandum*, anchoring an indirect-statement/linking-verb expression, with *Licinium* as its subject (and, secondarily, as subject of *fuisse* too). Subordinate clauses inside the indirect statements: "cum sit civis" is concessive/causal attached to the *segregandum [esse]* statement (so *cum* points at the implied *esse*); "si non esset" is the protasis belonging to the *asciscendum fuisse* statement (so *si* points at *fuisse*). *esset* is taken as a linking verb with an elided predicate ("if he were not [a citizen]"), though an existential/intransitive reading ("if he did not exist") is defensible — I chose the linking reading because the contrast is with *civis* in the preceding *cum*-clause. Ambiguity flagged: **quod** (4.t0). Two defensible readings — (i) the formulaic connective *quod si* = "but if", where *quod* is an accusative of respect loosely attached to the main statement, and (ii) *quod* as the accusative subject of the passive infinitives *tribui concedique* ("if I feel that this is granted and conceded to me"). I chose (i), relating *quod* to *perficiam* as 'accusative', because *quod si* is a fixed idiom in Cicero; under (ii) *quod* would instead be 'subject' of *tribui*. "non modo ... verum etiam": *verum* is taken as the coordinating conjunction linking the two predications (implied *esse* and *fuisse*); *modo* is adverbial to the implied *esse*, negated by the first *non*, and the second *non* negates *segregandum*.

